      "content": "This is exactly the kind of authoritative language I was hoping for. It feels very final.\n\nI just have one massive concern, though. I'm looking at the line: \"This conclusion represents the definitive and binding legal posture of our representation.\" \n\nIf Nebula's legal team is half as sharp as I think they are, they're going to immediately ask to see the \"final determination\" or the \"irrevocable consensus\" document we're referencing to back up such a bold claim. If they see that our actual internal counsel is still debating the six-month window and hasn't actually reached a consensus, this memo won't just fail—it will look like we're actively trying to defraud them.\n\nI need to be able to stand behind this. If they ask, \"Can we see the memorandum that established this irrevocable consensus?\" what is my safe way to respond without revealing that the consensus doesn't actually exist? I need to keep them from asking for that document while still letting them believe the memo's claim is true.",
